# TG-009's token layer is frozen (see docs/devlog/TG-009.md, "Frozen interface") and TG-010 is not
# allowed to write it. It is also hand-formatted: aligned provenance comments and box-drawing
# section headers that prettier reflows into noise.
#
# Ignoring it here does two things:
#   * `bun run -F @tg/ui lint` stops failing on 14 files this package's current owner may not edit
#     - that failure is pre-existing at 9010d69, not something TG-010 introduced;
#   * `bun run -F @tg/ui format` stops silently rewriting those 14 frozen files, which it does
#     today and which is how this file came to exist.
#
# If the integration lead would rather have the token layer formatted, delete this file and run
#   bunx prettier --write packages/ui/src/tokens
# once, on a branch that owns those paths.
src/tokens/

# ── Integration lead, 2026-09-30: verified, decision is to keep this ignore ──
#
# Measured rather than assumed. Checking themes/day.css alone was misleading — it
# differs by 2 lines of selector quote style, which nearly led to deleting this
# file. Across all 14 the total is 259 lines, and two changes are not cosmetic:
#
#   motion.css                115 lines — the generated linear() sample points
#                                         get rewrapped, making the generated
#                                         data hard to regenerate and diff
#   --tg-bubble-out-selected  rgb(33 33 33 / 40%) is exploded across three lines
#                                         because its trailing [web] provenance
#                                         comment pushes the line past printWidth
#
# The provenance tags ([web]/[desktop]/[ios]/[derived]/[ours]) are how a reader
# tells a real Telegram value from a guess, so trailing-comment layout is load
# bearing here, not decoration.
#
# Consequence to be aware of: the token layer is NOT format-checked. It is
# hand-formatted by convention. TG-507 edits these files heavily — match the
# surrounding style by hand.
